<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kediri Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kediri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kediri Kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> state in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> that flourished in the eleventh and twelfth centuries. Its rise, followed by that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, created a powerful rival centre in the region. This shift contributed to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kediri Kingdom" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Asia_trade_route_map_XIIcentury.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The kingdom grew out of the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>'s realm. In 1042, according to the tradition, Airlangga split Kahuripan into two parts, Janggala and Panjalu (Kadiri), and abdicated in favour of his sons to live as an ascetic; he died seven years later. The Marimbong Inscription of 1264 places the boundary between the two regions at the Bengawan River. In the early inscriptions the name Panjalu was used more often than Kediri. Kadiri comes from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> word meaning to stand alone or be independent. The capital, Daha or Dahanapura, is thought to have stood in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas River</a> valley, near the modern city of Kediri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jayabhaya (1135–1159) is remembered for a reign that is considered the golden age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> literature. Mpu Sedah's Kakawin Bharatayuddha, a Javanese version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, was written in 1135 and perfected by Mpu Panuluh, who also wrote the Hariwangsa and the Gatotkacasraya. Under Kameçvara, Mpu Dharmaja wrote the Smaradhana, and the story of the king and his wife Çri Kirana lies behind the Panji cycle, which spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> as far as Siam.</p><p>The last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertajaya">Kertajaya</a> (1194–1222), was fighting the brahmins in 1222, and they asked for protection from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ken-arok">Ken Arok</a>, ruler of Tumapel. The decisive battle took place near the village of Ganter, where Ken Arok's troops destroyed Kertajaya's. Kediri became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Tumapel, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>, under regents. In 1292 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> rebelled against Singhasari and killed its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a>. He rebuilt Kediri, but it lasted only a year before a combined attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a>, who later founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chinese sources describe a society based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation, animal farming and the spice trade. Daha lay inland near the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brantas-river">Brantas</a> valley, and Kediri inherited irrigation systems from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>'s Kahuripan, among them the Wringin Sapta dam. The economy was partly monetised, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins issued by the royal court. The Chinese author Chou K'u-fei, writing in 1178, reported that offenders paid fines in gold, except thieves and robbers, who were executed, and that the bride's family received a bride price.</p><p>A water festival was held in the fifth month and a festival in the mountains in the tenth, with flutes, drums and wooden xylophones. Chou Ju-kua, writing around 1225, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> as the two powerful kingdoms of the archipelago and found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> religion practised in Java.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kediri kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Majapahit Empire was established in the late 13th century in eastern Java after the collapse of the Singhasari kingdom, whose last king, Kertanegara, was killed by the rebel Jayakatwang of <b>Kediri</b> in 1292.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The rise of the Javanese kingdoms of <b>Kediri</b>, then Singhasari and Majapahit, created a powerful rival centre that contested Srivijayan cultural and commercial influence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Janggala was absorbed by <b>Kediri</b> by the early 12th century; an inscription of King Jayabaya of 1135 declares 'Panjalu jayati', Panjalu is victorious.</blockquote><a href="/state/janggala">Janggala</a></li><li><blockquote>Before retiring as an ascetic, Airlangga had his kingdom divided around 1045, tradition says by the sage Mpu Bharada, between two sons: Janggala in the east and <b>Kediri</b>, or Panjalu, in the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kahuripan">Kahuripan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1292 Jayakatwang of <b>Kediri</b> killed Kertanegara and set up Gelang-gelang.</blockquote><a href="/state/singhasari">Singhasari</a></li></ul></section>
